checks passed: 8    checks failed: 0

WHAT THIS PROVES

COMPLIANCE tier - the fine-tuned weights and the eval datasets:
  A specific version cannot be deleted. Not with a normal delete, not with a
  bypass, not with the root credential, not until the 30-day retention runs
  out. An overwrite becomes a new version and leaves the old one readable.
  Ransomware running with our own credentials cannot destroy the recovery point.

GOVERNANCE tier - the vector dumps, prompts and configs:
  The same delete is refused for an ordinary caller but allowed for a
  privileged one who passes --bypass. We keep that hatch deliberately. The
  trade-off is that a sufficiently privileged attacker could use it too, and
  that is exactly why the weights are NOT in this tier.

And the thing that is easy to get wrong: a plain delete on a versioned bucket
only writes a delete marker. Remove the marker and the object is back.
Immutability lives at the version level, not at the filename level.
